    {
      "id": "39545b52eb966fe84867bf2aafa54b8ef234f274",
      "individual_note": false,
      "resolvable": true,
      "resolved": true,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 3394764891,
          "type": "DiffNote",
          "body": "This solution causes the dissector fail to throw exceptions when it should.\n\nThe root of the issue is that casting `framelen` (an unsigned 32-bit integer) to a signed 32-bit integer can produce a negative number, rendering this comparison useless. Since the loop isn't entered, no exception is thrown by the `proto_tree_add_item` call on a very large `framelen`, and then the `offset += WS_ROUNDUP_4(framelen)` call undergoes signed integer overflow back to a smaller offset, resulting in an infinite loop.\n\n`try_dissect_next_protocol` always returns a value 0 or larger (and should return an unsigned eventually, but in practice it's always a value that fits in an int due to max frame and tvb sizes).\n\nA better fix for the local issue is:\n\n```\n            unsigned dissected = (unsigned)try_dissect_next_protocol(\n                parent, frame_tvb, pinfo, urb, urb_type, frame_tree, NULL);\n            if (dissected < framelen) {\n```\n\nThis causes the following `proto_tree_add_item` call to be hit, and that will throw the appropriate exception instead of looping. A bogus framelen *should* throw an exception.",
          "author": {
            "id": 6764551,
            "username": "johnthacker",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "John Thacker",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/99c30adacedf45514aaf711ba8a44f56322ebb53e9b550b7e3c9cd83bd6a1408?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/johnthacker"
          },
          "created_at": "2026-05-28T13:18:58.842Z",
          "updated_at": "2026-05-28T13:52:50.365Z",
